{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"content":"The user wants me to act as a \"Ratssitz\" (Council Seat) in the \"omegaflow\" house, answering three specific architectural questions based *only* on the provided context. I must follow a strict format: Verdict, Reasoning (2-3 sentences), Strongest Counter-argument/Riss, and \"pending\" status if uncertain with missing measurements. No invented facts. German language. Max ~700 words.\n\nLet's analyze each question based *only* on the provided text.\n\n**Question 1: R-2026-10-18-a: Receipt in Token-Ledger**\n*   Current state: Token state (xfer) only in Sender's Ledger. Receiver cannot acknowledge (`xfer close` on foreign token = hard error). Quit and Self-Completion both `CLOSED` (indistinguishable). `register_lookup --addressed` lists closed blocks; `--fired` stays 0, done reads as open. Previous `xfer ack` arm reverted (commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines).\n*   Options:\n    *   A: Own Act `ACK <tok> by=<line>.<seat>` in Receiver's Ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\n    *   B: Status quo: Receiver writes CLOSED in Emitter-Ledger.\n    *   C: No new Act: Receipt state derived read-only from Token-Ledger.\n    *   D: One Ledger per Token instead of per Line (both write same storage).\n*   Sub-question `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks vs S2 list with state column.\n\n*Reasoning for Q1:*\nThe problem is the receiver cannot acknowledge without error, and states are indistinguishable. Option B is explicitly the broken status quo. Option D changes the fundamental architecture (per-line ledger) which isn't suggested as a fix in the text, just an option. Option C (derived read-only) doesn't solve the \"receiver acknowledges\" action problem if the receiver needs to *act* to confirm receipt. Option A introduces a distinct Act `ACK` in the Receiver's Ledger, making `ACKED` distinguishable from `CLOSED`. This aligns with \"Selbst-Erledigung\" (Self-Completion) vs Quittung (Receipt). The reverted `xfer ack` suggests an attempt was made but rolled back, perhaps due to implementation details, but the requirement (distinguishable states, receiver agency) remains.\nThe `--addressed` sub-question: S2 (list with state column) is better because `--fired` stays 0 and done reads as open currently; a state column fixes the visibility without losing history.\n\n*Verdict Q1: A + S2.*\n*Riss:* The reverted commit (eb2f1b749) shows previous implementation failed/removed. Risk of re-introducing complexity.\n\n**Question 2: R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency Key `register-coverage`**\n*   Current: `group: register-coverage-${{ github.ref }}`, `cancel-in-progress: false`.\n*   Proposed: `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}`, `cancel-in-progress: true` (like `ci-gate` since 2026-10-08).\n*   Measurements: Push on main fires 5-7 Gates. Last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD-SHAs. In 10 mins: 8 `register-coverage` runs, 7 `cancelled`. In one concurrency group, new run deletes waiting predecessors; `cancel-in-progress: false` protects only the running one. Gate checks HEAD state of Register, no per-SHA artifact.\n*   Previous Round: 3:3 Tie. A per-SHA (HEAD defined per SHA). B per-ref (Ref is address of HEAD, per-SHA addresses past). Kimi practical: Cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state; Gate must ignore `cancelled`.\n*   Options: A per-SHA + true. B per-ref + true (only youngest run lives). C Debounce/Batch.\n\n*Reasoning for Q2:*\nThe Gate checks *HEAD state of Register*. HEAD moves with the Ref (branch). If I use per-SHA (Option A), I create a new concurrency group for every commit SHA. Since the Gate checks the *current* HEAD (Ref), running an old SHA's coverage is useless (addresses the past, as Fugu/Mistral/Kimi said). However, `cancel-in-progress: true` on per-SHA does nothing useful because they are different groups anyway.\nOption B (per-ref + true): Groups by Ref (e.g., `main`). `cancel-in-progress: true` cancels the *running* job if a new one starts on same Ref. The measurement says: \"in one concurrency group a new run deletes waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` protects only the running\". Wait. Standard GH Actions behavior: `cancel-in-progress: true` cancels *running* jobs in the same group. Queued jobs are not \"in progress\". If the group is per-ref, a new push queues a new run. If `cancel-in-progress: true`, it cancels the *currently running* one. The measurement says 7 cancelled in 10 mins. This suggests the current config (per-ref, false) queues them all up? No, \"in 10 Minuten acht Läufe, sieben cancelled\". If current is `cancel-in-progress: false`, why are 7 cancelled? Maybe manual cancellation or workflow collision?\nActually, the text says: \"in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden.\" This describes the *behavior of the proposed change* or a misunderstanding. Standard GH: `cancel-in-progress: true` cancels running. Queued runs remain queued unless `cancel-in-progress` applies to them (it doesn't usually).\nBut the core argument: Gate checks HEAD (Ref). Per-SHA is wrong because it checks old state. Per-Ref is correct target. `cancel-in-progress: true` on Per-Ref ensures only the latest push's run executes (cancels previous running run on same ref). This saves resources. Kimi's risk: \"Cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state\". But the Gate *reads* Register state (HEAD). It doesn't write Register state (it's a check gate). \"Das Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, kein je-SHA-Artefakt.\" So it's a read-only check. Cancelling a read-only check mid-way is safe (no inconsistent state written).\nSo Option B (per-ref + true) seems correct. It ensures only the latest commit on the branch runs the check.\n\n*Verdict Q2: B.*\n*Riss:* Kimi's warning about mid-write cancellation (though Gate is read-only, maybe it writes coverage report artifact?). Text says \"Gate prüft... kein je-SHA-Artefakt\".\n\n**Question 3: R-2026-10-18-e: Register knows not `state/stimmen`**\n*   `register_lookup` (Rust) searches `docs/{handover,surveys,plans,auftrag,blatt,concepts,paper}` — NOT `state/stimmen/` where all Rat-Verdicts lie (measured 2026-10-18: `grep -c stimmen` = 0).\n*   Consequence: Line asks \"what is open for me?\" and sees foreign verdicts only if Leitstelle distributes manually.\n*   Lines may not change Architecture themselves (R-2026-10-18-d/e: Architecture paths need RAT-ID).\n*   Verdict Format: `## An <line>` blocks at end of Verdict file.\n*   Options:\n    *   A: Add `state/stimmen/` as additional doc root in Reader.\n    *   B: Derive: Verdicts migrate as `## An <line>` blocks into living Handover of Receiver Line.\n    *   C: New Register Doc `state/stimmen/_register.md` (Index only) as own source.\n    *   D: Status Quo: Leitstelle distributes manually.\n*   Sub-question: Must the choice itself be in Index as RAT-ID before built?\n\n*Reasoning for Q3:*\nThe problem is discoverability of Verdicts (`state/stimmen/`) by `register_lookup`.\nOption A: Add path to Reader. Simple, direct, solves the search immediately. Verdicts are already formatted with `## An <line>`.\nOption B: Move/Copy blocks to Handover. \"Derive\" implies automation or manual process. \"Linien dürfen Architektur nicht selbst ändern\". Moving files might be architecture change? But Handover is `docs/handover` which *is* indexed. This creates duplication/sync issues.\nOption C: Index file. Adds indirection. `register_lookup` would need to parse the index then fetch files. More complex than A.\nOption D: Status Quo is the problem.\nThe format `## An <line>` blocks at end of Verdict file suggests the Verdict file *is* the source. Adding the directory to the Reader (Option A) makes them searchable immediately without moving files or building indices. It respects \"Lines don't change Architecture\" (Central change to Reader config).\nSub-question: \"Muss die Wahl selbst als RAT-ID im Index stehen, bevor sie gebaut wird?\" (Must the decision itself be in Index as RAT-ID before built?). R-2026-10-18-d/e says \"Architekturpfade brauchen eine RAT-ID\". Changing the Reader's search paths is an Architecture path change. So yes, the decision (this verdict) needs a RAT-ID to authorize the change to the Reader.\n\n*Verdict Q3: A.*\n*Zusatz: Ja, weil Architekturpfade (Reader-Konfig) RAT-ID benötigen (R-2026-10-18-d/e).*\n*Riss:* `state/stimmen/` might contain non-Register","reasoning":"The user wants me to act as a \"Ratssitz\" (Council Seat) in the \"omegaflow\" house, answering three specific architectural questions based *only* on the provided context. I must follow a strict format: Verdict, Reasoning (2-3 sentences), Strongest Counter-argument/Riss, and \"pending\" status if uncertain with missing measurements. No invented facts. German language. Max ~700 words.\n\nLet's analyze each question based *only* on the provided text.\n\n**Question 1: R-2026-10-18-a: Receipt in Token-Ledger**\n*   Current state: Token state (xfer) only in Sender's Ledger. Receiver cannot acknowledge (`xfer close` on foreign token = hard error). Quit and Self-Completion both `CLOSED` (indistinguishable). `register_lookup --addressed` lists closed blocks; `--fired` stays 0, done reads as open. Previous `xfer ack` arm reverted (commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines).\n*   Options:\n    *   A: Own Act `ACK <tok> by=<line>.<seat>` in Receiver's Ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\n    *   B: Status quo: Receiver writes CLOSED in Emitter-Ledger.\n    *   C: No new Act: Receipt state derived read-only from Token-Ledger.\n    *   D: One Ledger per Token instead of per Line (both write same storage).\n*   Sub-question `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks vs S2 list with state column.\n\n*Reasoning for Q1:*\nThe problem is the receiver cannot acknowledge without error, and states are indistinguishable. Option B is explicitly the broken status quo. Option D changes the fundamental architecture (per-line ledger) which isn't suggested as a fix in the text, just an option. Option C (derived read-only) doesn't solve the \"receiver acknowledges\" action problem if the receiver needs to *act* to confirm receipt. Option A introduces a distinct Act `ACK` in the Receiver's Ledger, making `ACKED` distinguishable from `CLOSED`. This aligns with \"Selbst-Erledigung\" (Self-Completion) vs Quittung (Receipt). The reverted `xfer ack` suggests an attempt was made but rolled back, perhaps due to implementation details, but the requirement (distinguishable states, receiver agency) remains.\nThe `--addressed` sub-question: S2 (list with state column) is better because `--fired` stays 0 and done reads as open currently; a state column fixes the visibility without losing history.\n\n*Verdict Q1: A + S2.*\n*Riss:* The reverted commit (eb2f1b749) shows previous implementation failed/removed. Risk of re-introducing complexity.\n\n**Question 2: R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency Key `register-coverage`**\n*   Current: `group: register-coverage-${{ github.ref }}`, `cancel-in-progress: false`.\n*   Proposed: `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}`, `cancel-in-progress: true` (like `ci-gate` since 2026-10-08).\n*   Measurements: Push on main fires 5-7 Gates. Last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD-SHAs. In 10 mins: 8 `register-coverage` runs, 7 `cancelled`. In one concurrency group, new run deletes waiting predecessors; `cancel-in-progress: false` protects only the running one. Gate checks HEAD state of Register, no per-SHA artifact.\n*   Previous Round: 3:3 Tie. A per-SHA (HEAD defined per SHA). B per-ref (Ref is address of HEAD, per-SHA addresses past). Kimi practical: Cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state; Gate must ignore `cancelled`.\n*   Options: A per-SHA + true. B per-ref + true (only youngest run lives). C Debounce/Batch.\n\n*Reasoning for Q2:*\nThe Gate checks *HEAD state of Register*. HEAD moves with the Ref (branch). If I use per-SHA (Option A), I create a new concurrency group for every commit SHA. Since the Gate checks the *current* HEAD (Ref), running an old SHA's coverage is useless (addresses the past, as Fugu/Mistral/Kimi said). However, `cancel-in-progress: true` on per-SHA does nothing useful because they are different groups anyway.\nOption B (per-ref + true): Groups by Ref (e.g., `main`). `cancel-in-progress: true` cancels the *running* job if a new one starts on same Ref. The measurement says: \"in one concurrency group a new run deletes waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` protects only the running\". Wait. Standard GH Actions behavior: `cancel-in-progress: true` cancels *running* jobs in the same group. Queued jobs are not \"in progress\". If the group is per-ref, a new push queues a new run. If `cancel-in-progress: true`, it cancels the *currently running* one. The measurement says 7 cancelled in 10 mins. This suggests the current config (per-ref, false) queues them all up? No, \"in 10 Minuten acht Läufe, sieben cancelled\". If current is `cancel-in-progress: false`, why are 7 cancelled? Maybe manual cancellation or workflow collision?\nActually, the text says: \"in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden.\" This describes the *behavior of the proposed change* or a misunderstanding. Standard GH: `cancel-in-progress: true` cancels running. Queued runs remain queued unless `cancel-in-progress` applies to them (it doesn't usually).\nBut the core argument: Gate checks HEAD (Ref). Per-SHA is wrong because it checks old state. Per-Ref is correct target. `cancel-in-progress: true` on Per-Ref ensures only the latest push's run executes (cancels previous running run on same ref). This saves resources. Kimi's risk: \"Cancel mid-write leaves inconsistent state\". But the Gate *reads* Register state (HEAD). It doesn't write Register state (it's a check gate). \"Das Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, kein je-SHA-Artefakt.\" So it's a read-only check. Cancelling a read-only check mid-way is safe (no inconsistent state written).\nSo Option B (per-ref + true) seems correct. It ensures only the latest commit on the branch runs the check.\n\n*Verdict Q2: B.*\n*Riss:* Kimi's warning about mid-write cancellation (though Gate is read-only, maybe it writes coverage report artifact?). Text says \"Gate prüft... kein je-SHA-Artefakt\".\n\n**Question 3: R-2026-10-18-e: Register knows not `state/stimmen`**\n*   `register_lookup` (Rust) searches `docs/{handover,surveys,plans,auftrag,blatt,concepts,paper}` — NOT `state/stimmen/` where all Rat-Verdicts lie (measured 2026-10-18: `grep -c stimmen` = 0).\n*   Consequence: Line asks \"what is open for me?\" and sees foreign verdicts only if Leitstelle distributes manually.\n*   Lines may not change Architecture themselves (R-2026-10-18-d/e: Architecture paths need RAT-ID).\n*   Verdict Format: `## An <line>` blocks at end of Verdict file.\n*   Options:\n    *   A: Add `state/stimmen/` as additional doc root in Reader.\n    *   B: Derive: Verdicts migrate as `## An <line>` blocks into living Handover of Receiver Line.\n    *   C: New Register Doc `state/stimmen/_register.md` (Index only) as own source.\n    *   D: Status Quo: Leitstelle distributes manually.\n*   Sub-question: Must the choice itself be in Index as RAT-ID before built?\n\n*Reasoning for Q3:*\nThe problem is discoverability of Verdicts (`state/stimmen/`) by `register_lookup`.\nOption A: Add path to Reader. Simple, direct, solves the search immediately. Verdicts are already formatted with `## An <line>`.\nOption B: Move/Copy blocks to Handover. \"Derive\" implies automation or manual process. \"Linien dürfen Architektur nicht selbst ändern\". Moving files might be architecture change? But Handover is `docs/handover` which *is* indexed. This creates duplication/sync issues.\nOption C: Index file. Adds indirection. `register_lookup` would need to parse the index then fetch files. More complex than A.\nOpti